---
/**
 * AboutUs — who is behind the work, stated once and shown once. Deliberately
 * the simplest beat on the page: one centred claim, one photograph, nothing
 * else. It sits between Projects (the proof) and Método Zil (the process), so
 * its job is to put people between the two, not to add another argument.
 *
 * SPACING — plain `py-section`, both halves, like any other flow section.
 *
 * The rhythm note in global.css says a section bordering a pinned one should
 * drop its padding on that side and let the pinned stage own the space, and
 * this was built that way first (`pt-section` only). It measured WRONG: the
 * gap below came out at 122px against the 160px every other seam on the page
 * uses, because Método Zil's stage is centred within `100svh` MINUS its own
 * `pb-28`, which leaves less headroom above its mark than the rule assumes.
 *
 * The rule's own example, Statement.astro, is not rendered on this page. The
 * section that actually bordered Método Zil before this one was Projects,
 * carrying a full `py-section` — so a half-gap here is what that seam has
 * always had, and dropping it is what made this one read tight. Match the
 * neighbour that is really there, not the one the note describes.
 *
 * THE CROP LIVES IN THE FILE, not in CSS. team.jpg is already cut to its
 * banner ratio (2439×1288, ~1.89:1), so there is no aspect box here and
 * nothing for CSS to trim: `w-full` plus the intrinsic dimensions Astro writes
 * render the file whole and reserve its space, so nothing shifts as it
 * decodes. That matters for this particular photo — its two groups of faces
 * sit near the left and right edges, so a CSS crop would eat people first.
 * Re-crop the asset rather than adding an aspect class.
 *
 * THE SOURCE IS THE MASTER THE DERIVATIVES COME FROM, so it is kept generous:
 * re-encoded at q92 with 4:4:4 chroma (~44dB PSNR, visually lossless) and NOT
 * resized, since 2439px is already near 2× the 1312px slot the photo fills on
 * a wide desktop. Astro re-encodes its webp derivatives FROM this file, so a
 * lossier master would compound its artifacts into every size the browser
 * actually downloads — that is why this is not squeezed harder.
 *
 * `widths` tops out at the source's own 2439px on purpose: Astro will not
 * upscale past it, so a larger entry only yields a duplicate candidate. If the
 * asset is ever replaced, re-check that number against the new file's width.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import teamPhoto from "../assets/img/team.jpg";
---

<section class="py-section">
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-8xl px-6 tablet:px-8 desktop:px-12">
    <div id="equipo" class="scroll-anchor"></div>

    <h2
      class="mx-auto max-w-[26ch] text-center text-h2 tracking-[-0.03em] text-balance"
      data-reveal
    >
      {
        /* The two words the section is actually about, lifted in `primary`.
          Plain <span>s inside the heading, so they stay part of one sentence
          for screen readers and for `text-balance`, which needs the whole
          string to measure. `visión estratégica` is deliberately NOT wrapped
          in nowrap — at 32px on a 375px screen it cannot fit one line, and
          forcing it would push the heading past the viewport. */
      }
      Un equipo comprometido que transforma marcas a través de <span
        class="text-primary">diseño y visión estratégica</span
      >
    </h2>

    {
      /* Its own `data-reveal` rather than a shared group with the heading: the
        photo is tall enough that a single trigger at the heading would run its
        entrance while the image was still well below the fold. */
    }
    <div class="mt-12 tablet:mt-16" data-reveal>
      <Image
        src={teamPhoto}
        alt="El equipo de Zil Design trabajando en el estudio."
        widths={[640, 960, 1312, 1920, 2439]}
        sizes="(min-width: 1408px) 1312px, 100vw"
        class="w-full object-cover"
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
